# Corner case: CROSS-BACKEND LIFECYCLE AGREEMENT (M1 canonicalization).
#
# An always-active event (importation, add(A, 100)) is coincident at t=5 with a
# theta-dependent intervention (transfer fraction=keep of A into B) that READS
# the compartment the event just modified. This is the one configuration where
# the within-substep effect order is observable: event-before-intervention vs
# intervention-before-event give DIFFERENT counts, because the transfer reads
# the post-event A.
#
# The transition rate is identically zero (drain @ k*A with k=0), so NO
# stochastic flow occurs on any backend and the only state change at t=5 is the
# event + intervention. This makes the post-substep counts integer-exact and
# hand-checkable, so chain_binomial / tau_leap / ode / gillespie must all agree.
#
# Hand computation of the canonical lifecycle (event from snapshot, applied
# BEFORE the intervention, which reads the post-event state):
#   start of step at t=5:                 A = 50,  B = 0
#   event  add(A, 100):                   A = 150, B = 0
#   intervention transfer 0.5*A, A -> B:  delta = floor(150 * 0.5) = 75
#                                         A = 75,  B = 75   (reads post-event A)
#   => A = 75, B = 75 at t >= 5
# The INVERTED (pre-M1) order would give transfer first (floor(50*0.5)=25:
# A=25, B=25) then add (A=125, B=25).
time_unit = 'days

compartments { A, B }

parameters {
  k    : rate        in [0.0, 1.0]
  keep : probability in [0.0, 1.0]
}

transitions {
  drain : A --> B  @ k * A
}

events {
  importation : add(A, 100) at [5]
}

interventions {
  pull : transfer(fraction = keep, from = A, to = B) at [5]
}

init {
  A = 50
  B = 0
}

simulate {
  from = 0 'days
  to   = 10 'days
}
